26.2  Where logs go: journald, syslog and /var/log

TL;DR
  - journald receives the logs. It keeps them on disk in /var/log/journal/ if
    that folder exists, otherwise in RAM under /run/log/journal/, lost at
    reboot.
  - A syslog daemon (rsyslog) may also copy them to text files: /var/log/syslog
    (Debian, Ubuntu) or /var/log/messages (older RHEL), and auth.log for logins.
  - Some programs write their own files: /var/log/nginx/, /var/log/apt/,
    /var/log/pacman.log.

      sudo mkdir -p /var/log/journal && sudo systemctl restart systemd-journald
      ls -lt /var/log | head
      sudo tail -f /var/log/nginx/error.log

On your machine
  Journal   on disk (/var/log/journal): kept across reboots · 300 B in 2 files
  Settings  SystemMaxUse=500M
  Syslog    rsyslog also writes text files in /var/log
  Rotation  2 rules in /etc/logrotate.d
  Biggest   /var/log/syslog 4.9 KiB · /var/log/nginx/access.log 2.9 KiB ·
            /var/log/auth.log 1.2 KiB

Pitfalls
  - Logs from before a crash need a persistent journal: create /var/log/journal
    (or set Storage=persistent) before you need them.
  - In containers there's often no journald: logs go to the container's output
    (docker logs).
  - /var/log/wtmp and btmp are binary login records: read them with last and
    lastb.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why both journald and rsyslog?

    journald arrived with systemd (2011); rsyslog was already everywhere, with
    remote logging, text files that tools expected, and filtering rules admins
    had written. Many distros kept both, journald forwarding to rsyslog. Recent
    ones increasingly drop rsyslog and rely on the journal alone, which can send
    logs to another machine itself.

  ▾ How do logs reach journald?

    Three ways: a service's standard output and error, captured by systemd; the
    syslog socket (/dev/log) that the syslog() call writes to; and journald's
    own API with structured fields. The kernel's messages are read from
    /dev/kmsg.

Related
  Commands  journalctl, tail, less
  Paths     /var/log/, /etc/systemd/journald.conf, /etc/rsyslog.conf
  Hub       atfq logs Logs
  See also  atfq 26.1 Reading the journal with journalctl
  See also  atfq 26.3 Log rotation and logs that fill the disk
